n you go  out and dj you might have a few beers  and you might sort of be a little bit  tipsy when you do a live stream you're  completely  stone cold sober and i was getting such  a buzz out of doing these live streams  and it was that that was my eureka  moment but wow music was what really  helped me through what was quite a  traumatic time in my life and then of  course the other thing i think you would  get the same answer out of anybody who  loses a parent early it makes you  realize it might all be gone tomorrow it  makes you realise the finality of life  and  it sort of kicks you into action  arguably faster than it would for others  who have a  slightly less sorry tale to tell at that  a similar age you are the first  generation of celebrity djs you were  voted the best dj in the world  let's just pause and enjoy that in 1995  by the dj mag what was it about the late  80s and 90s that gave rise to this  particular phenomena of the world famous  dj what was it about that time was it  quite a magical time it was a magical  time i mean it was  it's i i suppose what changed everything  and i don't want to sound like a luddite  because the internet or high-speed  internet  in many ways has has been the ultimate  um  propagator of all things artistic yeah  but before high-speed uh internet the  only way that you could discover this  incredible music this incredible vibe  was via a limited amount of touch points  which was kind of a handful of magazines  um record shops which specialist record  shops  a couple of shows on radio one and of  course these super clubs that existed in  many even quite small cities throughout  the uk  and i've always thought that the one  thing about clubbing stroke festival  culture is it's almost like the last  bastion of an opportunity to hang out  with people you don't know in a kind of  semi  for the most part a very safe  environment and jules the the the dance  music scene back then as we both know  was strongly associated to with hedonism  with partying of course with illegal  with illegal drugs  looking at you you are definitely not  one of those who are the casualty of  hard living may i say you're looking  very good  um was it unusual to stay removed from  all of that aspect of the lifestyle or  was it more common than maybe people  thought like you could be involved but  you didn't have to kind of partake in  the heavy drug taking yeah i think the  heart i think the hard party and  sometimes dare i say it is in the in the  people who hang around you know whether  they're djs or whether they're musicians  it's often the people who are hanging  around them rather than the artists  themselves because it's  the life yeah the life it's a job but  it's an it's a job that you're being  paid to do something that you would  willingly pay others to let you do but  at the same time it's a very hardcore  existence i was doing  two 200 250 shows a year probably 200  flights a year i still do not far shy of  that now but  going around the world doing a long haul  trip every every fortnight which sounds  like a on the one hand it's the dr you  know a schoolboy's dream on the other  hand you simply couldn't be absolutely  sort of uh  battered and bruised and do that it just  wouldn't work as it got more difficult  for musicians and artists to earn a  living because of  streaming and because of the way in  which the industry's changing it's got  more difficult to make a career for  yourself sticking your head above the  parapet is  has never been more difficult now there  are between sixty thousand and seventy  thousand spotify releases a day that's  nice um so you've gotta be an all-round  um  marketeer you've got a be business savvy  uh making good records just isn't enough  just on this ed sheeran was recently  awarded 900 000 pounds in legal for a  very high profile case after winning  that copyright talk in which he and two  of his co-writers were accused of  plagiarizing part of a song called a  